   },
   "source": [
    "Difference Between List and Tuple in Python\n",
    "\n",
    "| Feature     | List                        | Tuple                     |\n",
    "| ----------- | --------------------------- | ------------------------- |\n",
    "| Mutability  | Mutable (can change)        | Immutable (cannot change) |\n",
    "| Syntax      | `[ ]`                       | `( )`                     |\n",
    "| Performance | Slower                      | Faster                    |\n",
    "| Memory      | More memory                 | Less memory               |\n",
    "| Methods     | Many (append, remove, etc.) | Few (count, index)        |\n",
    "| Use Case    | Dynamic data                | Fixed data                |\n",
    "Lists are mutable and dynamic, while tuples are immutable and more memory-efficient, making tuples faster and safer for fixed data."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "id": "0e916906-45c8-4a2b-9e16-a9ff89c8e300",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "🔹 What is MCP in Agentic AI?\n",
    "\n",
    "MCP = Model Context Protocol\n",
    "\n",
    "It is a standard way for AI agents to access external tools, data, and context in a structured manner.\n",
    "\n",
    "🔹 Simple Explanation\n",
    "\n",
    "👉 MCP acts like a bridge between:\n",
    "\n",
    "AI model (LLM)\n",
    "\n",
    "External systems (APIs, databases, tools)\n",
    "\n",
    "So the agent can:\n",
    "\n",
    "- Fetch data\n",
    "\n",
    "- Use tools\n",
    "\n",
    "- Maintain context"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "id": "7517183d-ea7c-4ffc-90dd-68e00e148947",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Difference Between List and Array in Python\n",
    "\n",
    "| Feature      | List                           | Array                               |\n",
    "| ------------ | ------------------------------ | ----------------------------------- |\n",
    "| Built-in     | Yes                            | No (`array` module or NumPy needed) |\n",
    "| Data Types   | Can store different data types | Stores same data type only          |\n",
    "| Speed        | Slower                         | Faster                              |\n",
    "| Memory Usage | More memory                    | Less memory                         |\n",
    "| Flexibility  | More flexible                  | More efficient for calculations     |\n",
    "| Usage        | General purpose                | Numeric operations                  |\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "id": "b4dfcd36-ea58-4ef9-90d9-f9d662c39581",
   "metadata": {
    "vscode": {
     "languageId": "raw"
    }
   },
   "source": [
    "Question: \n",
    "How do you retrieve data using a pickled machine learning model stored in an AWS S3 bucket? Additionally, if you are receiving real-time data that is being stored in a database on AWS, how would you handle data processing and integration in that scenario?\n",
    "\n",
    "Answer:\n",
    "I load the pickled model from S3 using boto3 and deserialize it using pickle. For real-time systems, I don’t use S3 as a database;\n",
    " instead, I use RDS or DynamoDB for real-time access and keep S3 for storage and batch processing.\n",
    "  The model is loaded once in memory for low-latency predictions, and results or logs are stored back in S3."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "id": "5ab92a0c-df0c-4017-9dbc-5f3425629070",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "What is deep learning?\n",
    "A subset of ML using neural networks with multiple layers to learn complex patterns."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "id": "0bfcb220-0988-4d41-b6bf-a9e3731d6975",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "What is a neural network?\n",
    "A model composed of layers of neurons that transform input data."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "id": "b3a889bd-a916-46c0-89cb-6ee6439a3b5f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "What is backpropagation?\n",
    "Algorithm to update weights using gradient descent."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "id": "418f7c18-ea42-40cf-8de1-015d82922b51",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "What is gradient descent?\n",
    "Optimization technique to minimize loss."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "id": "d8b451a6-d30b-4a29-b237-c415d0587baf",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "What is vanishing gradient problem?\n",
    "Gradients become very small → slow learning."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "id": "8e478381-a91a-4f6a-a2cc-08c2db16f9d8",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "What is exploding gradient?\n",
    "Gradients become very large → unstable training."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "id": "1a8ad884-a4fc-4078-b1c7-be2ff2ea8a16",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "What is dropout?\n",
    "Regularization technique that randomly disables neurons."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "id": "c8ae1966-34ae-4f84-9b79-5b9a92c608e3",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "What is batch normalization?\n",
    "Normalizes inputs to stabilize training."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "id": "c19b8d6c-28f7-4c60-9f7f-a9461a03c97f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "What is activation function?\n",
    "Adds non-linearity (ReLU, sigmoid, tanh)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "id": "a6d474a6-cb17-45a0-a839-3618e165810c",
   "metadata": {
    "vscode": {
     "languageId": "raw"
    }
   },
   "source": [
    " what is multicollinearity? \n",
    " Multicollinearity means two or more independent features (X variables) are highly correlated with each other in a dataset.\n",
    "How to Handle:\n",
    "Remove one of the correlated features\n",
    "Combine features\n",
    "Use PCA\n",
    "Use regularization (Ridge/Lasso)\n",
    "    “Multicollinearity affects interpretability more than prediction” interpretability means trust, prediction is related to accuracy."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "id": "ed35c62a-0ab9-498f-89bb-1d8b6f7f94d6",
   "metadata": {
    "vscode": {
     "languageId": "raw"
    }
   },
   "source": [
    "what is overfitting?\n",
    "Overfitting happens when a model learns the training data too well, including noise and random patterns, and therefore performs poorly on new/unseen data.\n",
    "Training accuracy = very high ✅\n",
    "Test accuracy = low ❌\n",
    "🔥 How to Avoid Overfitting\n",
    "1️⃣ More Data\n",
    "More data → better generalization\n",
    "2️⃣ Regularization\n",
    "L1 (Lasso) → feature selection\n",
    "L2 (Ridge) → penalizes large weights\n",
    "3️⃣ Cross Validation\n",
    "Use k-fold validation for reliable evaluation\n",
    "4️⃣ Simpler Model\n",
    "Reduce complexity (depth, parameters)\n",
    "5️⃣ Dropout (Deep Learning)\n",
    "Randomly remove neurons during training\n",
    "6️⃣ Early Stopping\n",
    "Stop training when validation error increases\n",
    "7️⃣ Feature Selection\n",
    "Remove irrelevant features\n",
    "8️⃣ Data Augmentation\n",
    "Create more training data (images/text)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "id": "bd88038d",
   "metadata": {
    "vscode": {
     "languageId": "raw"
    }
   },
   "source": [
    "What is underfitting\n",
    "👉 Model learns too little\n",
    "Training accuracy → Low ❌\n",
    "Test accuracy → Low ❌"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "id": "10452548",
   "metadata": {
    "vscode": {
     "languageId": "raw"
    }
   },
   "source": [
    "| Feature           | Overfitting | Underfitting |\n",
    "| ----------------- | ----------- | ------------ |\n",
    "| Learning          | Too complex | Too simple   |\n",
    "| Training Accuracy | High        | Low          |\n",
    "| Test Accuracy     | Low         | Low          |\n",
    "| Generalization    | Poor        | Poor         |\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "id": "dbbc3500",
   "metadata": {
    "vscode": {
     "languageId": "raw"
    }
   },
   "source": [
    "2. Bias vs Variance\n",
    "\n",
    "🔹 Bias\n",
    "👉 Error due to wrong assumptions\n",
    "Model is too simple\n",
    "Misses true pattern\n",
    "📌 Example: Linear model for non-linear data\n",
    "\n",
    "🔹 Variance\n",
    "👉 Error due to too much sensitivity to data\n",
    "Model changes a lot with small data changes\n",
    "📌 Example: Deep tree memorizing data\n",
    "\n",
    "| Feature    | Bias             | Variance          |\n",
    "| ---------- | ---------------- | ----------------- |\n",
    "| Meaning    | Simplicity error | Sensitivity error |\n",
    "| Model Type | Simple model     | Complex model     |\n",
    "| Leads to   | Underfitting     | Overfitting       |\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "id": "e21dce72-a137-409b-a0f1-35d7f9fbb3bb",
   "metadata": {
    "vscode": {
     "languageId": "raw"
    }
   },
   "source": [
    "if i have 3000 features then how to choose right principle components? \n",
    "To choose the right number of principal components, \n",
    "I use the cumulative explained variance and select the minimum number of components \n",
    "that capture around 90-95% of the total variance. \n",
    "I also validate this using a scree plot to identify the elbow point."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "id": "b6dab512-1ecc-43c0-9bb8-31fca139e7dd",
   "metadata": {
    "vscode": {
     "languageId": "raw"
    }
   },
   "source": [
    " how you handle imbalanced dataset? how you do oversampling, undersampling, SMOTE?\n",
    "\n",
    " 🔴 Step 1: What is Imbalanced Data?\n",
    "\n",
    "Suppose dataset:\n",
    "\n",
    "Fraud (1)     → 10 rows  \n",
    "Normal (0)    → 90 rows  \n",
    "\n",
    "👉 Total = 100 rows\n",
    "👉 Problem: Model will learn → “Always predict 0”\n",
    "\n",
    "✔️ Accuracy = 90%\n",
    "❌ But model is useless\n",
    "\n",
    " | Method        | What it does           | Problem      |\n",
    "| ------------- | ---------------------- | ------------ |\n",
    "| Oversampling  | Copies minority data   | Overfitting  |\n",
    "| Undersampling | Removes majority data  | Data loss    |\n",
    "| SMOTE         | Creates synthetic data | Slight noise |\n",
    "\n",
    "🔵 Step 2: What is Oversampling?\n",
    "\n",
    "👉 Increase minority class (Fraud)\n",
    "\n",
    "🔹 Example (Random Oversampling)\n",
    "\n",
    "Before:\n",
    "\n",
    "Fraud = 10  \n",
    "Normal = 90\n",
    "\n",
    "After:\n",
    "\n",
    "Fraud = 90  (duplicated)\n",
    "Normal = 90\n",
    "\n",
    "👉 We copy fraud rows again and again\n",
    "🔹 Problem with Oversampling\n",
    "Duplicates data ❌\n",
    "Can cause overfitting\n",
    "\n",
    "-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
    "\n",
    "🔵 Step 3: What is Undersampling?\n",
    "\n",
    "👉 Reduce majority class (Normal)\n",
    "\n",
    "🔹 Example\n",
    "\n",
    "Before:\n",
    "\n",
    "Fraud = 10  \n",
    "Normal = 90\n",
    "\n",
    "After:\n",
    "\n",
    "Fraud = 10  \n",
    "Normal = 10  (removed extra rows)\n",
    "🔹 Problem with Undersampling\n",
    "Lose important data ❌\n",
    "\n",
    "------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
    "\n",
    "🔵 Step 4: What is SMOTE (MOST IMPORTANT)\n",
    "\n",
    "👉 SMOTE = Synthetic Minority Oversampling Technique\n",
    "\n",
    "👉 Instead of copying data:\n",
    "👉 It creates new fake (synthetic) data\n",
    "\n",
    "🔹 Simple Idea\n",
    "\n",
    "Suppose 2 fraud points:\n",
    "\n",
    "Point A = (100, 200)  \n",
    "Point B = (110, 220)\n",
    "\n",
    "SMOTE creates new point:\n",
    "\n",
    "New Point = (105, 210)  (between A and B)\n",
    "\n",
    "👉 So:\n",
    "\n",
    "No duplication\n",
    "\n",
    "🧠 Step 6: When to Use What?\n",
    "| Situation     | Best Method   |\n",
    "| ------------- | ------------- |\n",
    "| Small dataset | SMOTE ✅       |\n",
    "| Huge dataset  | Undersampling |\n",
    "| Quick fix     | Oversampling  |\n",
    "\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "id": "707bf68f-6e3c-4491-890b-52a503ac8fdd",
   "metadata": {
    "vscode": {
